Dabir and Egor were not satisfied with the fame from the previous episode, so they decided to make another TV show: the guys play their favorite game on an array a with their favorite integer k.
Dabir moves first. On the first move, any element from the array can be chosen and removed. Let the previous chosen element be equal to x. Then on the current move, except the very first one, a player must choose an element y from the array such that 0≤y−x≤k and remove it from the array. The player who cannot make a move loses.
But since this was not just a game, but a real show-match, Arseniy (aka MAKAN) — the main celebrity of Omsk was invited again. As a guest celebrity, Arseniy was given the opportunity to make the first move in this match, that is, to make the very first move in the game instead of Dabir. However, it turns out Arseniy is a fan of Egor, so he wants his first move to guarantee Egor a winning strategy against any response from Dabir.
Determine whether Arseniy can make the first move for Dabir so that, no matter how Dabir plays, Egor wins.

Input:
Each test consists of multiple test cases. The first line contains a single integer t (1≤t≤104) — the number of test cases.
The first line of each test case contains two integers n and k (1≤n,k≤2⋅105) — the length of the array and the favorite integer of Dabir and Egor.
The second line contains n integers a1,a2,…,an (1≤ai≤n).
It is guaranteed that the sum of n over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅105.

Output:
For each test case, if there exists such a first move that with optimal play by both players Egor wins, output "YES", otherwise output "NO".
You can output "YES" and "NO" in any case (for example, "yES", "yes", and "Yes" will be accepted).

Strategy:
Okay here we need to realize that since 0<=y-x<=k the previous number can never be smaller than the current number. Therefore the sequence of chosen number has to be non-decreasing. Another part is that duplicates are very important. Choosing a duplicate of a current number is the least constraining way to move since we dont go up. Because the order of the numbers in the input does not matter (just the value) we can compress it by creating a new array of pairs with {value, count}, we also keep it sorted to be able to easily assure the picked values are non-decreasing. The last thing is parity of the counts of the numbers. Since Dabir is starting but his first move gets picked by us, we play second. Since the numbers are non-decreasing we can calculate the possible states where we can win from the back. The most obvious one is an even count of the biggest number. This forces us to just pick the biggest number until the count is exhausted. We need to extend this down since the last count can be odd. We know we need to enter an even group second or an odd group first. That means that we should try to heavily go into odd groups. This translates to forcing dabir to start in a group that can reach an odd one that we could jump into and then there not being another odd group that dabir can escape to. If there is any even sized group in the array we can guarantee a win. This is because we can make dabir start there and he can either 1. stay which makes him lose, 2. leave to another even group which just makes him lose a bit later or, 3. theres an odd group in the range at which point we just go there first which makes us win too. There is one scenario where we would lose which is us jumping to an odd group and then dabir jumping into another odd group. We can ignore this though since if there was a possibility to jump into an odd group before we would've started there and therefore never got to this point. By going from the back and checking for an odd group to jump to, we jump the second there is one of them, therefore there can never be 2. We also track the last group we saw, if there is another group which sees this one (meaning the difference between the numbers <=k) we make dabir start at the earlier one and just jump into the odd one. This is the same as the logic described for the even group. Therefore the loop just goes from the end, tracks the last seen odd group number and checks if we could jump there from the current group, if so, we win. It also checks if the count is even. If it is we make dabir start there and we win too. We output a yes in any of these cases. If we finish without finding this we output no.